3. Matters noted

1. That the "Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities" is a protocol drawn up with the aim of further strengthening matters relating to protecting the rights granted to persons requiring special assistance by the "Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities", ratified by the Maldives on 5 April 2010, fully implementing that Convention, and establishing a system by which member states can be held accountable at the international level;

2. That it establishes a system for submitting a matter to the "Committee on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities", established under Article 34 of that Convention, where a state party to that Convention has taken away from a person or group of persons of that state a right or freedom protected and guaranteed under this Convention, and sets out the detailed procedures to be followed in that regard;

3. That the "Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities", an international human rights treaty, is a treaty that the Maldives ratified and joined on 5 April 2010, and that the Maldives has not submitted a reservation to any article of that Convention;

4. That an optional protocol setting out a system for submitting complaints to the UN "Committee on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities", by or on behalf of a party under the jurisdiction of that state, where a right set out in that Convention has been lost or taken away, and a system for that UN Committee to conduct inquiries in states, was passed by the UN General Assembly on 13 December 2006, and that the optional protocol came into force on 3 May 2008;

5. That although the Maldives ratified the Convention on 5 April 2010, the Maldives has not joined the optional protocol, and that, as a result, in the course of reviews of the Maldives under various international

[stamp] [signature]

human rights mechanisms, the Maldives has received many recommendations to ratify that optional protocol as soon as possible;

6. That the optional protocol is a protocol that establishes 2 important mechanisms for strengthening implementation of the Convention by the states party to the Convention:

• First mechanism: the mechanism for submitting complaints (individual communications) to the UN Committee by individuals or groups under the jurisdiction of a state that has ratified that protocol, if it is believed that a right set out in that Convention has been lost by or taken away from that individual or group, and if the procedures for seeking all domestic remedies or the legal stages provided by that state have been completed.

• Second mechanism: the mechanism for the UN Committee to conduct an inquiry in relation to an allegation, if a state that has ratified that protocol is credibly alleged to have violated a right set out in the Convention in a systematic manner and to a large extent.

7. That the protocol sets out the conditions for accepting complaints submitted under that protocol, the principles by which complaints will be accepted, and details relating to recommendations made by the UN Committee, and that, in addition, the protocol also sets out the procedural protection needed to protect those submitting complaints from retaliation;

8. That the optional protocol is a voluntary protocol, and that only states that ratify and join the protocol are required to comply with the principles of that protocol;

9. That out of the 192 states that have so far joined that Convention, 108 states have joined that protocol;

10. That although this is not mandatory, it is an important protocol in paving the way for accountability and the implementation of rights;

[stamp] [signature]

11. That, in accordance with Article 13 (2) of the Optional Protocol, the Maldives will be required to comply with that protocol only 30 days after the Maldives ratifies that protocol and submits the "instrument of accession";

12. That the power to withdraw after ratifying the Optional Protocol is granted under that protocol;

13. That, according to Article 2 of the Optional Protocol, it is stated that the Committee will not accept complaints submitted to the Committee in the following circumstances:

• The complaint being a complaint submitted in a way that does not reveal who the party submitting it is;

• The complaint being a complaint that abuses the right to submit complaints or that is incompatible with the articles of the Convention;

• The complaint having previously been examined, or being under examination at the time the complaint is submitted, by the UN Committee or by another international investigation or settlement mechanism;

• All the procedures for seeking the domestic remedies available in the member state in relation to the complaint not having been completed (however, this principle will not apply in circumstances where the provision of a domestic remedy in relation to such a complaint has been prolonged beyond what is reasonable, and where the likelihood of not obtaining a reasonable solution is high);

• The complaint not having evidence supporting it to a reasonable extent, or the matters stated in the complaint being matters without basis;

• The facts set out in the substance of the complaint having occurred before that state began to comply with that protocol (if the facts continued after the state began to comply with the protocol, the complaint may be accepted);

[stamp] [signature]

• That Article 4 of the Optional Protocol states that, after the UN Committee receives a complaint under that mechanism, and before a decision is made on the merits of the complaint, the UN Committee may send a request to the state to which the complaint relates asking that state to take measures that must necessarily be taken in order to protect the party who submitted the complaint from irreparable harm that may be caused to it;

• That when a complaint is submitted to the UN Committee, the Committee will bring it to the attention of the state to which the complaint relates, that a period of 6 (six) months will be given to respond to the complaint, and that the Committee will examine the matter in closed meetings, based on all the information submitted to the Committee; and

• That Article 7 of the Optional Protocol states that after examining the complaint, the Committee's views and recommendations will be sent to the member state to which the complaint relates, that the member state to which the complaint relates must respond in writing within 6 (six) months from the date of receiving the Committee's views and recommendations, and that, accordingly, when responding, it may be asked to share information on any measures taken by then in relation to the Committee's views and recommendations.

14. That under the inquiry mechanism in that protocol, if any member state is credibly alleged to have violated a right set out in the Convention in a systematic manner and to a large extent, the UN Committee is granted the power to conduct an inquiry in relation to that allegation;

15. That Article 6 (5) of the Optional Protocol states that inquiries conducted must be conducted confidentially, and that the cooperation of the state concerned must be sought at all stages of the entire inquiry;

[stamp] [signature]

• In addition, it is noted that these are not mechanisms newly accepted by the Maldives, that the Maldives has previously accepted these same kinds of mechanisms created under other international human rights treaties, that it is still being held accountable under those mechanisms, and that this work is being carried out by the Attorney General's Office.

19. That even if this protocol is ratified, it is not estimated that the State will incur any additional expense, because the work of being accountable under other such mechanisms is already being carried out by the Attorney General's Office, and because if the UN Committee visits the Maldives under the inquiry mechanism set out in the protocol, the UN will be responsible for that expense;

20. That it is not considered necessary for a specific law or regulation to come into existence in order to comply with this protocol once the Maldives ratifies it, because, although this protocol is a document with the status of a treaty, what this protocol opens to citizens is an additional door for submitting matters relating to their rights; because the requirements to be fulfilled in order to use that mechanism will be determined by the UN Committee; and because this protocol is not a treaty of the kind on which citizens are obliged to act, as set out in Article 93 (b) of the Constitution of the Republic of Maldives;

21. That joining this protocol will give additional strength to protecting the rights of persons with disabilities, and will pave the way for implementing the Convention in an appropriate and meaningful manner;

22. That the Maldives joining this protocol is not considered to have an adverse impact on gender equality, is not considered to have an adverse impact on the environment, that no specific obligation relating to publication and providing information has been noted, and that no matter has been noted requiring the public to bear additional expense or financial obligation;

[stamp] [signature]

it is noted that although this is not accepted [unclear], it is a protocol [?] being accepted [?] at the global level.

• In accordance with Article 13 (2) of the Optional Protocol, if the Maldives submits accession to this protocol, it is only 30 (thirty) days after the Maldives ratifies this protocol and submits the "instrument of accession".

• It is noted that the power to withdraw after ratifying the Optional Protocol is granted under this protocol.

The complaints mechanism set out in the Optional Protocol

According to Article 2 of the Optional Protocol, it is stated that the Committee will not accept a complaint submitted to the Committee in the following circumstances.

• The complaint being a complaint submitted in a way that does not reveal who the party submitting it is;

• The complaint being an abuse of the right to submit complaints or a complaint that is incompatible with the articles of the Convention;

• The complaint having previously been examined, or being under examination at the time the complaint was submitted, by the UN Committee or by another international investigation or settlement mechanism;

• The procedure for seeking all the domestic remedies available in the member state in relation to the complaint not having been completed (however, this principle will not apply in circumstances where the provision of a domestic remedy in relation to such a complaint has been prolonged [?] beyond what is reasonable, and in circumstances where the likelihood of not obtaining reasonable redress is high).

